WEBVTT

00:00.150 --> 00:15.950
Messier 35 or M35, also known as NGC 2168 or the Shoe-Buckle Cluster, is a relatively close open cluster of stars in the west of Gemini, at about the declination of the Sun when the latter is at June solstice.

00:16.542 --> 00:25.395
It was discovered by Philippe Loys de Chéseaux around 1745 and independently discovered by John Bevis before 1750.

00:25.925 --> 00:33.705
It is scattered over part of the sky almost the size of the full moon and is 2,970 light-years away.

00:34.427 --> 00:40.163
The compact open cluster NGC 2158 lies directly southwest of it.
